\section{Experiments and Results}
\label{sec:evidence}

\subsection{Skill-SP tool-call support and released-controller consequence}
API-Bank Level-1 has 314 covered rows, 183 with multiple memberships, and neutral $R^*=2$. The released pre-task sampler initializes all 15 tool-call packages at probability $1/15$, so a double-supported row receives $2/15$ eligibility versus $1/15$ for a singleton. The source paper reports a $-1.9$ point accuracy change when dynamic routing is replaced by uniform routing~\citep{huang2026skill}; we use this only to establish that allocation is operational, not to attribute utility to STRI.

For a same-state sampler-input reparameterization, we copy one active package with identical content, support, statistics, and sampler state under a fresh ID and rerun the author's weighting function. This is not a claim that the induction path would admit a literal duplicate. The cloned semantic class moves from $1/15$ to $2/16=1/8$, yielding prompt-mixture TV $=7/120=0.0583$ because package ID is absent from the questioner message. Quotient-conserved mass instead splits the original $1/15$ class mass and restores prompt TV exactly to zero. These are input-mixture statements, not generated-task or utility claims.

A frozen tool-disjoint test gives the same structural answer: calibration has 47 covered rows/33 multi-memberships and $R^*=2$; heldout has 52/38 and $R^*=2$. In the 4-tool$\to$4-tool split, calibration-fitted uniform, exact min-cover, max--min, and exact-$R^*$ weights retain heldout ratio 2, while inverse-support and NNLS transfer at 2.89 and 3.98. A stricter eight-fold leave-one-tool-out audit refits on seven tools and freezes weights for the eighth: uniform/exact-min-cover/exact-$R^*$ remain 2.0 on every fold, whereas NNLS has median 2.62 and worst 6.10. Thus the residual is not a single-split refitting artifact.

\subsection{Level-3: disjoint-support negative control}
Level-3 provides a natural released negative regime. Thirty-four rows are covered, every covered row has exactly one skill membership, and $R^*=1$. Equal weights on the active packages equalize every covered task. STRI-Cert therefore does not mechanically label every released taxonomy problematic.

\subsection{Logical compiler domain: high overlap but no residual}
The first-party logical release gives a stronger negative control: 128/128 programmatically generated units pass the author's compiler/uniqueness contracts and are cross-checked against all eight released support specifications, with no language model. Although 127/128 units have multiple memberships across 38 support patterns, $R^*=1$ because \texttt{skill\_008} supports every unit. This equalizer is concentrated: the max-share audit gives $R^*_{0.75}=4/3$, $R^*_{0.5}=2$, and $R^*_{0.25}=4$. High overlap is therefore not sufficient; realizability depends on support geometry and the declared feasible package-mass set.

\subsection{External graph analogues and SkillRL budget boundary}
SkillRouter's 75-query expert relevance graph (24 single, 51 multi) has uniform spread 7$\times$ but analogue $R^*=1$; including degraded relevant variants raises spread to 21$\times$ while remaining equalizable~\citep{zheng2026skillrouter}. AgentSkillOS supplies a complementary author-oracle-set graph: 20/30 tasks are multi-skill, the full graph has spread 4$\times$ and analogue $R^*=2.5$, yet category $R^*$ is 2 for data/document and 1 for motion/visual/web~\citep{li2026agentskillos}. These are relevance/oracle-set analogues, not executable support; omitted skills are not proven unsupported. Together they show that multi-membership and naive exposure spread alone do not determine realizability.

SkillRL supplies a complementary identity-sensitive retrieval seam. Across 223 ALFWorld descriptions and all 12 exact-clone targets, fresh-\texttt{dyn\_} priority changes semantic retrieval for 11/12 targets for every $k\in\{1,2,3,4,6,8,12\}$; unique-content crowd-out grows from 0 to 11 targets. A same-content non-dynamic-ID duplicate and exact-semantic quotient cause zero semantic-set changes at every tested budget. At $k=13$, where all 12 static contents plus the clone fit, the semantic-set effect vanishes; one extra slot at $k=12$ also restores it. This is a provenance-priority$\times$finite-budget phenotype, not an $R^*$ or utility claim; the separate qualified endpoint bridge is not a population-level no-effect theorem.

\subsection{AutoSkill: dynamic behavioral propagation}
AutoSkill~\citep{yang2026autoskill} exposes a first-party \texttt{sdk.search} seam. In one frozen five-skill P19 library at \texttt{top\_k=5}, a four-way exact-semantic split crowds out a post-checkout skill; ID renaming preserves, and exact-semantic quotienting restores, the original semantic set. The mechanical post-checkout behavior occurs in 6/6 original, 0/6 split, 3/3 placebo, and 3/3 quotient runs (one-sided Fisher $p=0.00108$). Under the split, adding the crowded-out skill back restores behavior in 3/3 fresh runs versus 0/3 for a matched cleanup add-back ($p=1/20$). This isolates one mediator on one substrate.

An outcome-blind preflight qualified 9/9 held-out units; SHA-256 selected two cross-scenario/position units before behavior. Across 8/8 valid A/B/C/D runs, neither met the frozen five-dimensional split-specific gate (A=B=C=D in one; A=B=C$\ne$D in the other), so repeat-2 and the remaining seven units were stopped. Retrieval sensitivity is not sufficient for task-general behavioral propagation; P19 remains bounded.

\subsection{Matched baselines, representation ablations, and failure analysis}
On frozen support, Level-1 uniform attains optimum 2; inverse-support reaches 98.33 (10.87 tempered), while NNLS lowers CV 0.312$\to$0.215 but worsens ratio to 5.51. Exact enumeration shows budgets 1--2 cannot cover Level-1; \texttt{skill\_003/004/015} attain unrestricted $R^*=2$ at budget 3, unchanged through 6. Level-3 needs four packages for $R^*=1$; universal \texttt{skill\_008} alone gives logical $R^*=1$. Thus average fit and controller sparsity do not explain the residual.

\paragraph{E4--E5: structural robustness.} Across 49 tools, all 4 closed-form-witness subsets are residual and all 10 overlap-without-witness subsets equalizable. Level-1 stays residual under 1,387 additions, 366 valid deletions, 184 tool-block$\times$package biases, 49/56 signature relabels, and 200 degree-preserving bipartite rewirings; logical breaks under 127/595 valid deletions. Exact neutral-target MILPs require at least 22 added cells or 71 deletions to reach $R^*=1$; 7/7 frozen target rays and all max-share tests remain residual. Peeling gives 22 pairwise-disjoint three-row witnesses over 66 rows/19 tools (21.0\% of Level-1) before $R^*=1$; package-wide overstatement is a boundary (3/9 residual). These are finite-snapshot controls, not randomization significance or witness uniqueness. \textbf{E6: conditional solver cost.} Dense SciPy/HiGHS is 0.00234 s at $128\times8$ and 0.685 s at $16{,}384\times96$ (max 0.765 s; 32,768 inequalities; 24.25 MiB), host-load-dependent with no asymptotic/solver-novelty claim.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.99\linewidth]{figures/stri-ablation-robustness.pdf}
\caption{Structural robustness. \textbf{A:} clone perturbation and quotient recovery. \textbf{B:} exact edit radii are 22 additions/71 deletions; 200/200 degree-preserving rewirings retain $R^*=2$. \textbf{C:} the residual survives 22 disjoint three-row witnesses spanning 19 tools. \textbf{D:} 7/7 frozen target rays remain residual and max-share $\rho\in[1/6,1]$ leaves $R^*=2$.}
\label{fig:ablation-robustness}
\end{figure}

\section{Discussion, Limitations, and Takeaway}
\label{sec:limitations}
Support geometry---not overlap/average fit---separates regimes: $R^*=1$ permits retuning; $R^*>1$ excludes same-information control; invalid provenance redirects to identification. AutoSkill keeps one bounded P19 proof, but its preregistered held-out pilot stops task-general propagation. The AutoSkill path and SkillRL phenotype neither supports population utility, safety, or regret; semantic-first remains constructive, not a validated repair.

